1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:05,013
How a Claim Travels The circulation of a striking story can outpace

2
00:00:05,026 --> 00:00:10,303
its verification In our project we examined the propagation of a claim

3
00:00:10,316 --> 00:00:15,724
across a cluster of accounts Its incidence increased sharply after one popular

4
00:00:15,737 --> 00:00:20,947
post but popularity did not establish truth We had to examine the

5
00:00:20,961 --> 00:00:26,987
representation of events and the possibility of fabrication The analysis was subject

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,039
to clear limits A missing record could make a conclusion invalid while

7
00:00:32,053 --> 00:00:37,368
an apparently uniform pattern might conceal distinct groups We could not assume

8
00:00:37,382 --> 00:00:42,513
that people responded uniformly or invariably shared the same motive Nor did

9
00:00:42,526 --> 00:00:47,789
a negative comment prove hostility We distinguished reach from penetration into a

10
00:00:47,803 --> 00:00:52,724
particular audience A story could be seen by many people yet understood

11
00:00:52,737 --> 00:00:58,079
by few The result was a useful lesson in public consciousness exposure

12
00:00:58,092 --> 00:01:04,447
does not automatically produce understanding Before deciding what a message achieves researchers

13
00:01:04,461 --> 00:01:08,132
must ask who encountered it how they interpreted it and what evidence

14
00:01:08,145 --> 00:01:09,566
supports the conclusion
